WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.160
[Narrator:] In 1900 bubonic plague was&nbsp;
epidemic in the&nbsp;ports of Asia.&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:18.160
Its mode of transmission was not understood. When&nbsp;cases surfaced in San Francisco the Chinese people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:24.400
were held accountable. Chinatown was cordoned off. Homes and businesses were fumigated and destroyed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:30.480
Plague was termed an "Oriental disease" peculiar to "rice-eaters" and moves were made to quarantine all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.160
of the Chinese. The city health officer also&nbsp;laid smallpox at the feet of what he called,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:42.400
"the unscrupulous lying and treacherous Chinamen" in their dark and dingy quarters. Leprosy, too&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:48.800
was considered an inherently Chinese disease,&nbsp;transmitted to whites via opium pipes. Fear and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:55.180
prejudice intensified by uncertainty, can easily&nbsp;override sympathy for those affected by such a&nbsp;disease. 

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.240
[Female Patient:] Everybody thought I was going to die and&nbsp;all them doctors and everything was around my bed.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.080
I remember the priest giving me last rites and&nbsp;you know I prayed and prayed to, you know....

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.600
Don't take me out right now because I still want&nbsp;to see my family grow up and my grandkids and all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:22.240
that, you know. They came to tell me about going up&nbsp;to the ward where they keep those people, you know.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.440
And I didn't want to go up&nbsp;
there because I was thinking, God&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.960
people gonna know. I don't want nobody to&nbsp; know, because people don't understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.720
about this sickness. They just treat&nbsp;
you like you got a plague or something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:46.880
You cannot be yourself. You can't let nobody know.&nbsp;You have to live a life, a very secretive life.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:56.480
[Male Patient:] Well, I thought I got it from a female. Which I still think to this day. The lady was a prostitute, okay,

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.800
and I felt that I'd come in contact with it&nbsp;that way, because she did tell me this, you know.&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.560
That she had tested positive. I was whisked away&nbsp;to a very far corner of the jail.  I was isolated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.480
away from everyone. I was not allowed contact&nbsp;with anyone. I was not allowed to use the phone.&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.800
I had to slip a message out to have someone&nbsp;call the American Civil Liberties Union and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.624
have an attorney come see me. So I could&nbsp;get out of the cell.  

00:02:19.624 --> 00:02:27.600
[George Simons:] Shanti Project this&nbsp;is George. Have you ever used, uh Shanti services&nbsp; before? Okay, you want to hold on a moment please?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:31.342
Shanti Project, can you hold please?  

00:02:31.342 --> 00:02:37.293
[Narrator:] In Hindu&nbsp;scripture, Shanti is a word for celestial&nbsp;peace. Sometimes repeated as an incantation.

00:02:37.293 --> 00:02:42.029
[George:] Shanti&nbsp;Project, can you hold please? Thank you for waiting, may I help you?

00:02:42.029 --> 00:02:47.040
[Narrator:] At the Shanti Project in San Francisco, dispensing celestial peace can be nerve-wracking.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:52.620
The organization is a counseling center&nbsp;for people with AIDS.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:58.503
[George:]  We're averaging around&nbsp;6,000 calls a month and I answer them&nbsp;all.

00:02:58.503 --> 00:03:01.360
[George:] Shanti Project, this is George. 

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:10.080
Jana,&nbsp;hi, this is, it's an intake please. He's a little&nbsp;distressed. Sometimes it's difficult and you just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:16.400
kind of have to um hope that there's a pause so&nbsp;that you can kind of take a deep breath and go on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.200
Because sometimes you hear things&nbsp;
that you know are a little unsettling.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.864
[Charles Herrington:] Yes, thanks.

00:03:32.864 --> 00:03:43.158
[Narrator:] To the Shanti staff, funeral&nbsp;arrangements, emergency places to live, and suicide&nbsp;counseling are everyday requests. Callers are&nbsp;often distraught. 

00:03:43.158 --> 00:03:51.157
[Charles:] Is your will a simple affair?&nbsp;In other words, are we dealing&nbsp; with, what is now a real basic estate?

00:03:51.915 --> 00:03:56.960
[Narrator:] Many of the diseases that strike&nbsp;people with AIDS, such as pneumocystis pneumonia,&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:02.400
can be grim in their later stages. Shanti&nbsp;counselors do not advocate suicide, but will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:08.480
refer people to the Hemlock Society which provides&nbsp;how-to information about voluntary euthanasia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.440
[George:] Well I just had a call from somebody that I&nbsp;know who it is, and he doesn't realize who I&nbsp;am.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:20.897
And um, he's asking for, well, Neptune Society&nbsp;and Hemlock Society. [Staff member:] You gonna call him back? &nbsp;

00:04:20.897 --> 00:04:25.760
[George:] No...the funny part of it is I really feel like&nbsp;I have to treat it as a confidential thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.120
[Staff:] You know the individual socially? [George:] Umhmm,

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.795
[Staff:] You could approach him from a social angle 

00:04:38.795 --> 00:04:45.003
[George:] I&nbsp;think that's what I'll do. If he's willing to wait&nbsp;for information about the Hemlock Society, he's&nbsp;
probably not planning to do anything right away.&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:56.468
[Staff:] Do I know who it is? [George:] Probably. [Staff:] I don't want to&nbsp;know. 

00:04:56.468 --> 00:05:04.480
[Man speaking to group:] You know, many people with AIDS or many&nbsp;people that are going through the dying&nbsp;process are obnoxious and very demanding...

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:11.840
[Narrator:] Hundreds of people come to Shanti as&nbsp;volunteers. The public in general is fearful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:17.760
people with AIDS, and that fear is often compounded&nbsp;by a lack of sympathy springing from the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:24.320
the virus is spread mainly among homosexuals and&nbsp;drug addicts. Most of these volunteers, however, &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:31.200
know someone who has AIDS or someone who has died, or&nbsp;have themselves tested positive for the virus. 

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:36.000
They are training to help patients and their families&nbsp;through the emotional turmoil of the disease.&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:42.720
[Speaker to group:] It's also very difficult when you prepare yourself&nbsp; for your loved one to die and then they don't die.&nbsp;

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:47.840
You know, the respirations start to slow down&nbsp;and you think oh god you know, it's gonna be 

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:53.440
maybe in 48 hours. The next day it's even worse and&nbsp;you think well maybe it's going to happen and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:06:00.240
you totally give them that last amount of your energy and then suddenly the next morning &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:09.040
they rally, they're feeling good and you think, oh my god,&nbsp;how am I going to do this for another two months?

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:16.240
You know and that's hard stuff to talk about. &nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:20.000
[Narrator:] At&nbsp;these training sessions one of the instructors is&nbsp;Bobby Reynolds, a native San Franciscan. 

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:27.501
His parents&nbsp; are dead and his only close relative is a sister. In 1982 his doctor diagnosed him&nbsp;with Kaposi's Sarcoma, Skin Cancer,

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.791
one of the primary diseases that strike people&nbsp;with AIDS. 

00:06:31.791 --> 00:06:39.720
[Bobby:] I expected that he would tell me&nbsp;&nbsp;everything was fine over the phone, when in fact he&nbsp;said I'm sorry, it's positive.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:50.820
So I went into shock. Living with AIDS these past four years has been&nbsp;an incredible experience. I treasure the lessons&nbsp;that I have learned.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:55.840
[Narrator:] Today Bobby Reynolds is a&nbsp;
rarity. The life expectancy of a person with AIDS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:02.480
is about 18 months, but chemotherapy successfully&nbsp;controlled his cancer. He has become a prominent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:08.160
advocate of the rights of people with AIDS, all&nbsp;the while mindful that with his immune system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:17.440
damaged by the virus, his body is defenseless&nbsp;against any disease that may come along. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:26.160
[Bobby:] What is, right now is I'm doing pretty good, and what's&nbsp;possible is a lot, you know, there's a lot possible.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:33.760
[Speaker 2:] When I was first diagnosed, people gave me, you&nbsp;wouldn't believe some of the stuff, coffee enemas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:40.800
[Laughter]

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:46.560
[Bobby:] They need someone to hang their hope on, they&nbsp; need to to have somebody to be a role model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:51.232
for them and I think I have been able to do&nbsp;that. &nbsp;

00:07:51.232 --> 00:07:58.960
[Speaker 2:] And so beyond dealing with your decision about how you want to be, you end up having to&nbsp; deal with all this other stuff that's out there.&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.880
[Susan:] I told my brother before I got pregnant that, you&nbsp;know, I wasn't sure how things would be between us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:12.800
because I didn't want to take any chances&nbsp;with my kids. Every doctor I talked to said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:16.400
that I shouldn't have any contact with&nbsp;
my brother because they just don't know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.800
[Rodney:] It was the kind of thing, well if&nbsp;nobody can tell us what's going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:26.168
then uh gee you know, what are you supposed to do?&nbsp;

00:08:26.168 --> 00:08:33.040
[Narrator:] Although they live just a few miles apart, Bobby&nbsp;and Susan have scarcely seen each other in the&nbsp;two and a half years since her twin boys were born.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:41.200
[Susan:] I told him that I would try and keep in touch&nbsp;with him on the phone which I did, and every week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:48.880
it would be like he'd get a little cooler and we&nbsp;finally just more or less cut off communications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:56.800
He told me that it was like I had rejected him&nbsp;and he just couldn't deal with it right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.840
and I couldn't do much about it because I figured&nbsp;hey you know, I'm pregnant, I'm not gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:11.040
get pregnant again, I can't take any chances. I don't&nbsp;want anything to happen to my baby.

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:17.040
[Bobby Reynolds:] Hundreds if&nbsp;not thousands of people have heard me speak and&nbsp;were able to look at AIDS through different eyes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.600
And I have touched men in support group and&nbsp;I have touched their families and I have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:32.320
able to help them find some ways of dealing and&nbsp;and you know, she's my sister.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:40.480
[Rodney:] He'd like Sue to be&nbsp;able to just jump and go, but... [Susan:] With three kids and&nbsp;Rod, I can't, it's just not possible... &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:45.840
[Rodney:] I mean, it's family first and unfortunately he's&nbsp;family too but it's immediate family 

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:49.517
and&nbsp;then it's the rest of the family. 

00:09:49.517 --> 00:09:58.081
[Susan:] I realize that they say casual contact, you probably wouldn't get AIDS, but they&nbsp;can't guarantee it. 

00:09:58.081 --> 00:10:04.400
You know, as much as&nbsp;my mind says hey it's okay, my heart says&nbsp;well it may be okay, but I still don't know.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:11.200
[Bobby:] You know I've never told my sister&nbsp;that she was wrong for being afraid,&nbsp;

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:18.640
but some of her fears are irrational and&nbsp;if she's going to continue being afraid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:31.840
there's some day that I may die and she will&nbsp;never have been able to cope with those fears.&nbsp;

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:39.200
[Narrator:] This woman was diagnosed with AIDS last year. She is addicted to heroin and believes she contracted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:44.400
the disease through shared needles. She would not&nbsp;allow her name or voice to be used on the air. 

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:51.360
But these are her words. [Female patient 2:] Well it hurts, you know, because&nbsp;you're carrying around a burden. You know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:56.320
what's going on and you can't let anybody know&nbsp;about it because you know if they know then you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:01.840
going to be an outcast. Well they're very hateful&nbsp;about it, "oh you got AIDS, don't get around me!"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.840
Oh I got in-laws I wouldn't even think of letting&nbsp;them know, you know. That they're not stupid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:14.560
people, they're not street people either. They're&nbsp;intelligent college people. 

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:20.560
That's the reason I&nbsp;don't want to come on the media. I don't want to&nbsp;get nothing that will hurt my family.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:26.720
 [Larry Saxon:] The majority&nbsp;&nbsp;of the clients I've worked with will not have any&nbsp;kind of media presentation done on them at all 

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:31.600
and I don't blame them. I understand perfectly&nbsp;well with the Larouche Initiative pending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:38.160
with that mentality floating around America,&nbsp;the potential quarantine, the potential lock-up&nbsp;mentality.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:46.000
I certainly understand them wanting to&nbsp;be secretive with their diagnosis.[ Male Patient 2:] Not only family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:54.080
and friends of concern for me, but the concern and&nbsp;worry for themselves, you know and the very selfish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.080 --> 00:12:01.600
attitudes, you know. Then I hope you see this. [ Sean O'Neil:] That's&nbsp;one great thing I must say about having AIDS, 

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:08.240
is you can really get away with saying anything. [Woman:] Is&nbsp;that right?   [O'Neil:] Yeah, like an old person or a child.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:14.480
[Narrator:] Sean O'Neil, is a writer and a lecturer on&nbsp;dance. He is also performed professionally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:18.080
Last summer he made an appearance&nbsp;
with the dance troupe in Sapporo, Japan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:25.840
[Sean O'Neil] After about a week I started feeling, not feeling&nbsp;right and having mainly just difficulty breathing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:33.040
Thinking back on it now, I cannot believe that I kept kept dancing and kept working. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:39.520
a matinee. It was an afternoon and I knew&nbsp;that I couldn't do the performance because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:45.360
in rehearsal, I couldn't finish the ballet,
on my own breath.  So I didn't know what to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:51.840
One of the girls on the company's dad&nbsp;was a doctor and he got us an oxygen tank.

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:05.280
As the music's beginning as, 'Rossini', is beginning,&nbsp;I hear them pull the plug on the oxygen. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:11.040
in the first movement I danced with four&nbsp;different women, four different times and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:15.520
so I did have a couple of exits on the first&nbsp;movement. Where I would just run off stage and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:20.720
immediately go under the the oxygen and try&nbsp;to get my breath back before I went back out.

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:28.880
I would be under the the tank and and I would&nbsp;see all of these little Japanese girls with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:32.080
their, you know, all wide-eyed just&nbsp;
staring at me. Like you know, is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:37.200
how Americans have just to get through&nbsp;a performance? So I just was very numb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:41.440
and then in a daze and just kind of you&nbsp;
know kind of floated my way through it.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:52.320
On the flight home, there was the Newsweek and Time&nbsp;articles about AIDS. I read this case history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:58.160
of this guy who had had pneumonia and every&nbsp;symptom that he had, I had. I went straight to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:04.800
my doctor and he diagnosed me as having pneumonia&nbsp;and AIDS, virtually there in his office on the spot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:12.880
[Carolyn, Sean's mother:] I didn't know that much about AIDS. I of course&nbsp;had heard about it. He was very very ill.

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:18.800
The first time he was hospitalized for two weeks and&nbsp;looked for a while like he might not make it. &nbsp;

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:27.668
So all we were concerned about at that point, was to&nbsp;get him over the pneumonia and worried about the battle against AIDS later.

00:14:27.668 --> 00:14:35.200
[Narrator:] Sean's family took turns&nbsp;staying with him. Friends have helped pay his rent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:40.240
Unlike most people with AIDS, he is free to&nbsp;concentrate on his own thoughts and feelings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:44.800
[Sean O'Neil:] The reactions that I got from my friends&nbsp;essentially saved me. [Sean and Friend:] "I should, I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:51.840
I should go for it."  [Sean O'Neil:] When you feel like you're close&nbsp;to dying. You just, it's the most unbelievable, um, feeling.

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:59.840
You just don't have any time for&nbsp;any baloney. I really started reading an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:05.440
awful lot and listening to tapes&nbsp;and meditating and all of that.

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:12.720
It makes you really look at your life and&nbsp;
at yourself and at your priorities and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:19.760
what you value. It's nice, it's really&nbsp;
nice to to be liberated from a lot of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:25.840
stuff. A lot of the day-to-day um, you know&nbsp;things you think are important that aren't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:32.320
[Female Patient:] Nobody really knows where that sickness came&nbsp;from. Oh you know, they're just learning about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:36.800
it. But you know, naturally they have to blame it&nbsp;on something. So they want to say, "oh you know, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:42.640
you're gay, you get it. You know. I'm no more gay&nbsp;than flying to the moon and I got it, you know.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:49.440
[Male Patient:] The beasties, the beasties, all of&nbsp;us to some degree have the beasties.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:57.200
You wake up in the middle of the night. How am&nbsp;I going to make my mortgage payment? Um, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.200 --> 00:16:00.800
am I going to say to my boss tomorrow? I really&nbsp;would like a raise and I don't know what to say.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:10.080
For me it's fear. When am I going to die? Last&nbsp;Friday, I started noticing a difficulty swallowing.

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:19.040
It got progressively worse over the weekend,&nbsp;to the point where I didn't want to eat and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:25.760
then it became sort of painful all the time.&nbsp;So tomorrow morning I have to go in for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:28.640
an endoscopy. Where they're going to insert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:35.840
a tube down my throat into my esophagus&nbsp;and stomach to see what might be going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:42.160
[Narrator :] Lynn Eubanks, is a pediatric nurse she also&nbsp;volunteers for the Shanti Project. She took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:46.800
her training from Bobby Reynolds and is now&nbsp;assigned to him as an emotional support counselor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:51.520
She went to see him when he was waiting&nbsp;to find out the cause of his sudden pains.&nbsp;

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:58.560
[Lynn Eubanks:] I was real scared. I didn't, I didn't want&nbsp;it to be the start of a decline. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:05.600
Bobby's done without for four years. I know that that day will probably come. But

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:10.960
I didn't want it to be now. I
won't want it to be whenever it is.

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:21.760
"Hi look at you," you're getting&nbsp;
ready for the party. [Bobby:] I am.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:28.400
[Narrator:] The initial test results were a surprise and a&nbsp;relief the pains appeared to stem from an ulcer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:33.920
[Lynn Eubanks:] Look great. [Bobby Reynolds:] That's all I've been&nbsp;
doing actually since I got over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.480 --> 00:17:40.400
the shock of it was laughing. When I call&nbsp;people to tell them that all the test results were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:45.840
negative and that they're treating me for an ulcer.&nbsp;They all say, "oh great it's only an ulcer,"... [Chatting] [Lynn:] We're done.

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:57.840
[Narrator:] At about the same time Bobby Reynolds became sick&nbsp;this summer,  Sean O'Neill contracted pneumocystis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:03.360
pneumonia for the second time. His mother, Carolyn, flew to San Francisco from the East Coast for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:09.120
the three week duration of his treatments. [Carolyn:] He&nbsp;was doing quite well until he had the sinus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:14.800
operation. Which kind of knocked him down and&nbsp;and put them wide open for the pneumonia again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:27.840
But we'll get through this one too. I'll&nbsp;
see you upstairs. [Sean:] Okay, all right boys. [Carolyn:] Good luck.

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:39.200
[Chatting] [Carolyn:] To wake up every morning and know you have to go&nbsp;
in there. The veins are getting more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:46.080
sore. They're starting to roll. They can't get&nbsp;it with the first try and it's just terrifying.

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:52.800
Last night he was extremely upset,&nbsp;
thinking about two more weeks of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:58.480
and so I wanted to ask you is there any&nbsp;
possibility of shortening the treatment or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.480 --> 00:19:04.560
is that out of the question? [Dr. Stephen Mihalko:] I would not recommend&nbsp;it, because if we do a shortened treatment there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:10.880
a very high possibility that will get a relapse.&nbsp;
[Narrator:] Dr. Stephen Mihalko, treats both Bobby Reynolds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:15.200
Sean O'Neill. [Dr. Stephen Mihalko:] So if we want to really treat&nbsp;this and try to keep it at bay for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:20.480
then we really should finish a standard&nbsp;
course of treatment and not cut any corners.

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:29.040
[Carolyn:] He's so good about this. That you can't&nbsp;be any less than he is. You can't be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:33.200
a whimpering fool and you have to&nbsp;
forget your own pain and your own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:37.840
fear and help him deal with his,&nbsp;
because he's the one who's sick.

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:41.013
Elevator floor]

00:19:42.815 --> 00:19:49.822
[Nurse, tapping and looking for vein]

00:19:52.240 --> 00:20:05.840
[Sen O'Neil:] You just do what you have to do to get through it.&nbsp;
The intravenous treatments were they, were horrible!

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:23.440
[Carolyn:] The first time we just thought, well we're&nbsp;different we're going to lick this thing. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:31.200
you know, the other patients seemed so tired and&nbsp;there was a resigned look in their eyes that just...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:37.360
We said well this isn't going to be us you&nbsp;know. Now as time goes on, you start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:42.720
understand how difficult it is to live&nbsp;
with this thing month, after month and um.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:52.000
Sean, said he can understand now, why anyone&nbsp;just doesn't want to fight it anymore. [Narrator:] While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:57.120
treating Sean O'Neill for pneumonia, Dr. Mahalka&nbsp;was running further tests on Bobby Reynolds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.120 --> 00:21:02.800
His ulcers were not as benign as they appeared.&nbsp;
[Dr. Mahalka:] Well actually it did turn out to be herpes. We got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:09.680
a culture report back yesterday and started him on&nbsp;anti-herpes medicine, uh, in an oral form. It can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.680 --> 00:21:14.720
serious and that can spread to other areas. But the&nbsp;main part of this is, it's associated usually with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:18.960
an incredible amount of pain and people are not&nbsp;able sometimes to even swallow their own saliva.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:27.920
[Bobby Reynolds:] So here it is now like a week and a half&nbsp;after that started and I'm feeling pretty good.&nbsp;

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:34.640
But I'm still having fevers at night&nbsp;
and I'm starting to have night sweats.

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:41.600
[Narrator:] Until last year Bobby counted on his lover&nbsp;Mark, to see him through the bouts of sickness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:47.440
and the fear of dying from AIDS. They had been&nbsp;together for eight years but Mark died last fall.

00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:54.960
[Bobby Reynolds:] Mark, Mark, Mark, he had his good&nbsp;points and bad points as we all do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.960 --> 00:22:00.080
He was a little arrogant. He was very&nbsp;
strong-willed about certain things.

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:03.040
But we had fun.

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:11.200
[Narrator:] When Bobby was diagnosed in 1982, Mark, was&nbsp;apparently healthy. But in April of 1985 he too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:17.520
was diagnosed, struck by cancer and a debilitating&nbsp;paralysis the disease progressed quickly in Mark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:26.000
[Mark:] I truthfully admit that I miss being able&nbsp;to go to restaurants and go to a movie. I mean I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:32.320
just not mobile enough to do that and those things&nbsp;are still important. I want to work, you know. I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.320 --> 00:22:40.400
to go in my kitchen and cook. I want to be able to&nbsp;help him work on the backyard... [Bobby:] When I tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:46.400
to prepare myself as much as possible because I&nbsp;knew his death was inevitable. But you really can't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:54.080
He went into respiratory failure and he had done&nbsp;that twice before. So I called just automatically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.080 --> 00:23:01.920
for the ambulance, the paramedics. But he was&nbsp;gone. He was the most important person in my life&nbsp;

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:09.600
for a long time. I think that says it all for&nbsp;anybody no matter what your sexual preference is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:16.160
You have somebody in your life that's the&nbsp;most important person, That's who he was,

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:21.840
is.

00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:25.120
What's next?

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:35.600
So I'll live it and I'll breathe it and I'll&nbsp;
eat it for so long and then I'll say, "break time."&nbsp;

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:39.840
You know, let's, let's go blow bubbles.&nbsp;
Let's go read a murder mystery or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:45.680
let's just unplug the phone and&nbsp;
forget about it. Luckily I can do that.

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:53.600
[Narrator:] Most people with AIDS don't have the help and&nbsp;support counted on by people like Bobby Reynolds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:59.200
and Sean O'Neil. This man is a construction&nbsp;worker with ARC or AIDS-related conditions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:05.600
He was diagnosed while serving a prison term last&nbsp;year. [Construction Worker:] Well their reaction was medieval. The deputies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:12.800
coming by myself, peering in the window calling&nbsp;me a disease carrier. Uh, you know, like I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:17.680
said. They're just, their whole overall attitude&nbsp;is very medieval, you know. It's like a witch or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:21.680
something, you know. What the hell? [Narrator:] When he&nbsp;arrived in San Francisco, this man went to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:27.840
the AIDS Foundation which assigned social worker&nbsp;Larry Saxon to the case. Saxon's job is to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:34.480
people with AIDS and ARC get out of the worst of&nbsp;situations. [Larry Saxon:] they may have an AIDS or ARC diagnosis&nbsp;

00:24:34.480 --> 00:24:39.600
and they also are suffering from alcoholism and&nbsp;drug addiction. It's the most unpopular population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:45.680
you can possibly imagine. A lot of those guys&nbsp;are also racial minorities. [Narrator:] The AIDS Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.680 --> 00:24:50.480
rents apartments at undisclosed locations&nbsp;to house destitute people with AIDS. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:56.640
they try to arrange for social security, medical&nbsp;and a place to live. [Larry Saxon:] Their lovers throw them out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.640 --> 00:25:01.360
Landlords throw them out. Friends, roommates, decided&nbsp;they can't live with them anymore because they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:04.960
afraid that they're going to come down with AIDS.&nbsp; Because they're sleeping under the same roof with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.960 --> 00:25:10.960
them. Which is insane! But people cater to that&nbsp;psychology of fear. They tend to go a little crazy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.960 --> 00:25:18.720
[Male quoting movie:] "D-mil, I'm ready for my close-up. Oh yes." [Larry Saxon:] They're&nbsp;all very independent, extremely independent. Most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:27.840
them have not been on welfare before. [Same male who quoted movie:] Don't worry&nbsp;about it. If it don't kill you it'll make you fat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:37.920
[Man in the crowd:] Well, here at the Mill Valley Rotary Club. [Introducing Presenter:] Today&nbsp;we're honored to have with us as our speaker Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:45.040
Mary Reddick, who is the executive director of&nbsp;the Marine AID Support Network. [Narrator:] As AIDS spreads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:50.560
beyond the commonly known risk groups, gay men and&nbsp;intravenous drug users. All segments of society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:55.760
have a stake in learning about the disease. The&nbsp;fact that the AIDS virus is only transmissible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.760 --> 00:26:00.720
sexually or through the blood is not always&nbsp;reassuring news. [Dr. Mary Reddick:] One of the things that I learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:06.640
in doing AIDS work, was I learned that there's&nbsp;no such thing as a closed sexual boundary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:13.120
About half of the gay men in the United States,&nbsp;sleep with a woman at least once a year. We run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.120 --> 00:26:19.920
the hotline at the Marine Aid Support Network and&nbsp;we know the kind of the level of fear. [Woman Patient:] I was in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.920 --> 00:26:26.320
hospital. I'll never forget, this a nurse was taking me&nbsp;out and I didn't have the sickness then. Walking me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:31.600
down and saying mean things about homosexuals&nbsp;and how they got the sickness and how they,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:37.920
you know, "God's punishing them." I said to myself,&nbsp;damn and she goes, "well I go and take care of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:43.120
patients." But she has no feeling for them and I&nbsp;thought what if she was at my bedside when I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.120 --> 00:26:51.840
dying and didn't have no compassion and thought&nbsp;I should be punished? But I've been dead, you know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:58.000
[Narrator:] The epidemic has become a political phenomenon.&nbsp;Candlelight Memorials in San Francisco and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:09.200
elsewhere have turned into rallies against&nbsp;discrimination AIDS is a frightening disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:13.760
But to those who have it, public opinion&nbsp;is frightening and threatening as well.

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:23.760
[Bobby Reynolds:] People's fears are real. But they may not&nbsp;understand what my lifestyle is like. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:29.280
they understand that I'm a person.&nbsp;
That I'm hurting. So that they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:33.840
send me off, to help just because I'm&nbsp;
a gay man or just because I have AIDS.

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:44.080
Together the citizens of San Francisco will&nbsp;continue making a difference together. Together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:53.920
my friends, together for we are a family and we are&nbsp;not alone. Keep looking for the rainbows. Thank you.

00:27:57.520 --> 00:28:01.840
Narrator: The AIDS virus has already claimed&nbsp;more than a thousand San Franciscans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:05.520
Thousands more will join the&nbsp;
unwilling ranks of, 'those people'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.520 --> 00:28:12.640
before there is even hope for a cure. [Bobby Reynolds:] With us in&nbsp;this moment of healing you may say the names of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:20.800
those who have died or those who are now living&nbsp;for whom your heart hurts. Let us begin first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.800 --> 00:28:28.240
with the names of the three men who began this&nbsp;candlelight march three years ago, Bobby Campbell (we open our hearts),

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:57.840
William Calderon (we open our hearts) and Jerry Luponey

00:29:18.160 --> 00:29:25.840
Richard (we open our hearts). ...

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:33.220
you

